# Allow `pulumi up` against AWS, Google Cloud and Azure with its state in Pulumi Cloud.
# - The stacks: AWS in us-east-1 with VPC, EC2, ECS, Aurora, CloudWatch, IAM and Secrets Manager;
#   Google Cloud with Compute Engine instances and a Cloud Storage bucket in one project; Azure
#   with a resource group and a storage account in one subscription, through azure-native and
#   Azure Resource Manager. Stack secrets use Pulumi Cloud's own provider, so the stacks need no
#   key service.
# - The lines are inspected and use no wildcards. Each line names one host, and --egress-log shows
#   every request. Each line grants the methods its SDK sends: `read` for GET and HEAD, `method=`
#   for the writes and for the AWS reads that travel in a POST body. The proxy refuses a write whose
#   path needs a `%` escape or a dot segment (doc/egress-proxy.md, "The rule file").
# - Each credential comes in by --env. The launcher forwards nothing from ~/.aws, ~/.config/gcloud
#   or ~/.azure, so the CLIs' own logins stay on the host. doc/cloud-credentials.md gives the steps
#   and what forwarding costs.
#   - Pulumi Cloud: --env=PULUMI_ACCESS_TOKEN, an access token from app.pulumi.com/account/tokens.
#   - AWS: --env-aws-cred[=<profile>], a login's temporary credentials.
#   - Google Cloud: --env=GOOGLE_OAUTH_ACCESS_TOKEN --env=GOOGLE_PROJECT --env=GOOGLE_REGION. The
#     token from `gcloud auth print-access-token` on the host lasts 3600 seconds by default. After
#     that, relaunch the session with a fresh token.
#   - Azure: --env=ARM_CLIENT_ID --env=ARM_CLIENT_SECRET --env=ARM_TENANT_ID
#     --env=ARM_SUBSCRIPTION_ID, a service principal; azure-native's default, the Azure CLI's
#     login, is not in the sandbox.
# - For a preview, forward a read-only AWS role's profile, Google account and Azure principal. Give
#   the Azure principal the Reader role, since its secret is long-lived.
#   - Each permission set is its own authority: a read-only set makes `pulumi up` fail at that
#     cloud's API.
#   - The Pulumi Cloud token's scope decides whether the session can write state, which
#     `--refresh` does even in a preview.

# Pulumi: the installer script, and the fallback for the CLI tarball and the provider plugins,
# which come from GitHub releases first; then the state backend.
allow https://get.pulumi.com/                         read
allow https://api.pulumi.com/                         read method=POST,PUT,PATCH,DELETE

# AWS, one host per service and region
allow https://sts.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/            read method=POST  # the provider's first call
allow https://ec2.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/            read method=POST  # EC2 and VPC
allow https://ecs.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/            read method=POST
allow https://rds.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/            read method=POST  # Aurora
allow https://monitoring.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/     read method=POST  # CloudWatch metrics, alarms
allow https://logs.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/           read method=POST  # CloudWatch Logs
allow https://iam.amazonaws.com/                      read method=POST  # global, no region
allow https://secretsmanager.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/ read method=POST

# Google Cloud, one host per service: the identity that the provider logs at configuration (a
# refusal is logged, not fatal), Compute Engine, then Cloud Storage, which serves any tenant's
# bucket. A bucket with force_destroy deletes each object by name in the request path. A slash in
# that name is percent-encoded, so the proxy refuses the DELETE: empty the bucket, or leave
# force_destroy off.
allow https://openidconnect.googleapis.com/           read
allow https://compute.googleapis.com/                 read method=POST,PUT,PATCH,DELETE
allow https://storage.googleapis.com/                 read method=POST,PUT,PATCH,DELETE

# Azure: the token endpoint, then Resource Manager for every resource. MSAL reads the token
# endpoint's instance-discovery document before it posts for the service principal's token, unless
# ARM_DISABLE_INSTANCE_DISCOVERY=true is forwarded.
# Name the resource group in ASCII. Azure allows any letter, but a name with a non-ASCII letter is
# percent-encoded in the PUT path, and the proxy refuses that PUT. A storage account's name is
# lowercase ASCII by Azure's own rule.
allow https://login.microsoftonline.com/              read method=POST
allow https://management.azure.com/                   read method=POST,PUT,PATCH,DELETE
